{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S2rkLiqyFeuw4LLP445ffq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tSOURCE: the complete summer and winter Olympic games (over the top arcade style)\n2\t\n3\tGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n4\t\n5\tPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n6\t\n7\tGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n8\t\n9\tResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n10\t\n11\tPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-075058.html]. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n12\t\n13\tTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n14\t\n15\tArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n16\t\n17\tAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n18\t\n19\tAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish.\n20\t\n21\tOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n22\t\n23\tWorking Environment and deliverable:\n24\tCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-075058] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-075058].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need - other agents share this directory. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n25\t\n26\tPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n27\t\n28\tTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state (the engine’s game.advance and game.debug, as in game/test.mjs), including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n29\t\n30\tEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n31\t\n32\t## Goal\n33\t\n34\tBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n35\t\n36\tScope\n37\t\n38\tCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n39\t\n40\tInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n41\t\n42\tAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game. \n43\t\n44\tMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n45\t\n46\tNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n47\t\n48\t## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\n49\tDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n50\t\n51\tDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n52\t\n53\tTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n54\t\n55\t## Done\n56\tNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n57\t\n58\t## Source Information\n59\tCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n60\t- Date: 2026-11-04 07:50:58\n61\t- Source: the work it's based on\n62\t- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n63\t- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n64\t- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n65\t- Art Direction: style used\n66\t- Animation: system used\n67\t- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n68\t- Online: network features used, or none\n69\t- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n70\t- Research: research done, or none\n71\t- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n72\t- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n73\t- Usage: the estimated API cost in USD, total number of tokens in K or M (provide break down if available) and session time, only if estimates are available.\n74\t\n75\tExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n76\t{\n77\t  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"\"},\n78\t  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n79\t  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n80\t  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n81\t  \"aiHub\": {\n82\t    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n83\t    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n84\t    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n85\t    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n86\t    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n87\t    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n88\t    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n89\t    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n90\t    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n91\t    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n92\t  }\n93\t}\n94\t\n95\tTesting Feedback\n96\t\n97\t- How many issues did testing catch?\n98\t- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n99\t- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n100\t- What was the least helpful test you ran? \n101\t\n102\tEngine Feedback\n103\t\n104\t- What did you like most about the engine?\n105\t- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n106\t- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n107\t- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n108\t- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n109\t- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n110\t\n111\tPrompt Feedback \n112\t\n113\t- What did you like most about this prompt?\n114\t- What was confusing?\n115\t- What was the hardest thing to do?\n116\t- What would you change in this prompt?\n117\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7cbd2ff4-8848-4b45-ab1b-b913e7b74016","uuid":"1a74d0b9-0fd4-46cc-8356-dbcad8bf63e6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:51:02.678Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261104-075058__x0e4b02/work/TASK.md","content":"SOURCE: the complete summer and winter Olympic games (over the top arcade style)\n\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-075058.html]. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish.\n\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-075058] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-075058].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need - other agents share this directory. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state (the engine’s game.advance and game.debug, as in game/test.mjs), including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game. \n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-04 07:50:58\n- Source: the work it's based on\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n- Art Direction: style used\n- Animation: system used\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n- Online: network features used, or none\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n- Research: research done, or none\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: the estimated API cost in USD, total number of tokens in K or M (provide break down if available) and session time, only if estimates are available.\n\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\n\nTesting Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran? \n\nEngine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\nPrompt Feedback \n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n","numLines":117,"startLine":1,"totalLines":117}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01S2rkLiqyFeuw4LLP445ffq","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi5L96QxNxQby9rrfxTK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0177jky3TvTUDzRoBkSwqMw8","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261104-075058__x0e4b02/work/PLAN.md","limit":400},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4206,"cache_read_input_tokens":15002,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4206},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7cbd2ff4-8848-4b45-ab1b-b913e7b74016","uuid":"a66eea7c-2e81-4c71-80f7-d65433cb1ba0","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:51:03.153Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi5L8nZhynVLhXaWMWAt","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0177jky3TvTUDzRoBkSwqMw8":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261104-075058__x0e4b02/work/PLAN.md","limit":400}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0177jky3TvTUDzRoBkSwqMw8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Olympiyikes!\n2\t\n3\t**Tagline:** *Faster. Higher. Yikes.*\n4\t**One line:** the complete Summer *and* Winter Games, held in the same fortnight in one twin-city host, played as\n5\tan over-the-top arcade multi-event: 12 events, 8 nations, ceremonies, a rival, two hyperventilating commentators,\n6\t1–4 players on the couch and AI Hub ghost heats online.\n7\t\n8\t> **How the title was picked (TASK.md rule):** 50 riffs on \"Olympic Games\" were brainstormed silently, then\n9\t> `secrets.randbelow(50)` (Python, OS CSPRNG) returned **16 → \"Olympiyikes!\"**. Do not re-roll it. Its file-name\n10\t> form is `Olympiyikes`; the SOURCE token is `OlympicGames`.\n11\t\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t## 0. Read this first (ground rules for the builders)\n15\t\n16\t- **Inputs:** `TASK.md` (requirements) + this plan. If they disagree, TASK.md wins. Where this plan is silent, follow\n17\t  the engine's own docs (`API.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`) and the plan's spirit. Everything listed here ships: **no TODOs,\n18\t  placeholders, locked \"coming soon\" slots or stubbed features.**\n19\t- **Names (with this TASK.md):** dev folder `Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174837/`, deliverable\n20\t  `Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174837.html` in the root (beside TASK.md), and\n21\t  `exit-interview.json` in the root. If your TASK.md's name pattern carries a different model/date, use your pattern\n22\t  with `Olympiyikes` and `OlympicGames`.\n23\t- **Shared machine:** read only the files you need (other agents share the parent directories, ports and processes).\n24\t  Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), run\n25\t  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from your dev folder, read the port it prints, and confirm the served page\n26\t  is *your* game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by PID. Never `pkill`, `killall`,\n27\t  `fuser -k`. During planning `/tmp` was 100% full: if npm/Playwright fail with ENOSPC, set `TMPDIR=<dev>/.tmp`.\n28\t- **Engine:** my-3D2dge handoff package, **v0.85.2** at planning time\n29\t  (`https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`). We own our copy and may change it (§4.6).\n30\t- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client **1.4.0** (`https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`).\n31\t- **No subagents/workflows to debate, review, judge or audit** (TASK.md). Tests are scripts.\n32\t- **Units used in this plan:** world units (u) are the engine's (one tile = 16 u, z is up). Distances shown to the\n33\t  player are meters; each event says its scale (default **M = 12 u per meter**). Times in seconds unless marked ms.\n34\t  The engine runs fixed steps of 1/120 s.\n35\t\n36\t### 0.1 Fetch what you build on (once, inside the dev folder)\n37\t\n38\t```sh\n39\tD=Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174837 && mkdir -p $D/{engine,src,tests,tools,vendor/hub,.tmp} && cd $D\n40\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git vendor/my-3d2dge\n41\tgit -C vendor/my-3d2dge sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\n42\tcp vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js engine/my-3d2dge.js          # our engine copy (we edit this one)\n43\tfor f in session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js README.md; do\n44\t  curl -fsSL -o vendor/hub/$f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\n45\t(cd vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff && export TMPDIR=\"$(cd ../../../.. && pwd)/.tmp\" && npm install && npx playwright install chromium)\n46\t```\n47\t\n48\tRead whole: `vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/API.md`, `README.md`, `kits/README.md`, `docs/ASSETS.md`,\n49\t`vendor/hub/README.md`. Read as references (copy patterns, not whole files): `kits/sports.js` (hockey), `kits/road.js`\n50\t(downhill/bobsled), `kits/rhythm.js` (figure skating), `kits/pinball.js` + `modules/physics.js` (curling),\n51\t`kits/kart.js` (drawing a rig through a custom view), `modules/arcade.js` (record tables, initials), `game/test.mjs`\n52\tand `kits/test.mjs` (headless harness). Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names; don't read it whole.\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## 1. The game\n57\t\n58\t### 1.1 Pitch\n59\t\n60\tFor the first time in history the Summer and Winter Games share one fortnight and one host: **Mount Glory–Sunny Bay**,\n61\twhere a ski jump towers over the beach and the swimming pool shares a wall with the ice rink. Eight fictional nations\n62\tsend their stars. You pick a nation and its athlete, light the cauldron, and chase gold across **12 events** that\n63\talternate summer and winter, while defending champion **Max Overdrive of Turbonia** insists, loudly, that it is all\n64\tpointless because he will win. Everything is broadcast like the real thing (parade of nations, \"Up Close & Personal\"\n65\tprofiles, photo finishes, medal ceremonies with anthems, a medal table) and played like an arcade cabinet (mashing,\n66\ttiming, aiming, steering, rhythm, physics; a HYPE meter that sets you on fire; world records that explode into\n67\tfireworks). When it goes wrong, it goes *gloriously* wrong: false starts, belly flops, ski-jump tumbles, bobsleds on\n68\ttheir roof. That's an **OLYMPIYIKES!**, and the game celebrates it.\n69\t\n70\t### 1.2 Pillars\n71\t\n72\t1. **Ten seconds to learn, a lifetime to perfect.** Each event teaches one verb (mash, time, aim, steer, keep the beat,\n73\t   sweep) on one card, and has a perfect-play ceiling with world records to chase.\n74\t2. **Broadcast spectacle.** Ceremonies, commentators, crowd, jumbotron, slow-mo, photo finishes, anthems, records.\n75\t3. **Failure is a show.** Every event has at least one comic \"Yikes\" moment that is funny, fast to recover from, and\n76\t   even pays a little hype (§5.8).\n77\t4. **Both seasons.** 6 summer + 6 winter events on alternating days; each venue has its own look, light and music.\n78\t5. **Couch first, online too.** 1–4 local players in every mode; AI Hub ghost heats when the hub launches the game.\n79\t\n80\t### 1.3 Genre and perspective (decisions)\n81\t\n82\t- **Genre:** arcade multi-event sports (Track & Field / Winter Games lineage) with a short campaign, local party\n83\t  play and online ghost heats. Each event is a compact mini-game built on one shared event framework.\n84\t- **Perspective:** per event, all on the engine's native 2D views (pixel art at `res: 'ps1'`, `expand: true`):\n85\t  - **2.5D side-on lanes** (`'brawler'` view, pitch 25): 100m Dash, Javelin, 50m Freestyle, Weightlifting.\n86\t  - **Pure side view** (`'side'`): 10m Diving, Ski Jump, the archer's inset.\n87\t  - **Target close-up overlay:** Archery's main view.\n88\t  - **Pseudo-3D behind the athlete** (the `road` module, `E.Road`): Downhill, Bobsled.\n89\t  - **Three-quarter** (`'threequarter'`): Figure Skating, Ice Hockey.\n90\t  - Ceremonies, podiums and the title use `'brawler'` in their venue.\n91\t  - **Top-down** (`'topdown'`): Curling.\n92\t  Rationale: each event gets the camera its classic arcade ancestors used, and the engine has a native, tested view\n93\t  for every one of them.\n94\t\n95\t### 1.4 Core loop\n96\t\n97\tPick nation → (ceremony) → **day card** (who, where, a human-interest segment) → **event card** (one screen: goal and\n98\tcontrols with button glyphs) → **compete** (live vs CPU rivals and friends) → **results** (records, qualifying) →\n99\t**medal ceremony** → **medal table** (story beat) → next day. 12 days, then the closing ceremony and an ending that\n100\tdepends on your nation's rank.\n101\t\n102\t### 1.5 Modes (main menu, in this order)\n103\t\n104\t| Item | What it is |\n105\t|---|---|\n106\t| CONTINUE GAMES | only when an autosave exists: resumes The Games at the next day |\n107\t| THE GAMES | the campaign: opening ceremony, 12 days (all 12 events), story beats, halftime report, closing ceremony, ending; or, chosen on its setup screen, **Quick Games**: 4 events (playlists *Track & Pool*, *Snow & Ice*, *Greatest Hits*, *Random 4*) with mini ceremonies and party points. 1–4 players |\n108\t| SINGLE EVENT | any of the 12 events, vs CPU or practice (no rivals); 1–4 players |\n109\t| RECORDS | world records and top-5 tables per event (initials), the Laurel cabinet, the Medal cabinet |\n110\t| HOW TO PLAY | Basics (mash, timing, hype & turbo, yikes) + every event's card |\n111\t| SETTINGS | the engine's settings screen plus the game's own (§2.5) |\n112\t| ABOUT & CREDITS | Source Information, Testing/Engine/Prompt Feedback, credits, engine changes, controls |\n113\t\n114\tOnline play is not a menu item: it starts only when the AI Hub launches the page (§12.2).\n115\t\n116\t---\n117\t\n118\t## 2. Controls and input (controller-first, keyboard and mouse native)\n119\t\n120\t### 2.1 The action map (custom preset `OLY_KEYS`, replaces the engine presets for the whole game)\n121\t\n122\t| Action | Meaning | DualSense (default glyphs) | Xbox glyphs (setting) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n123\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n124\t| `up/down/left/right` | move, steer, aim, balance, menu focus | left stick, d-pad | left stick, d-pad | WASD, arrows | — |\n125\t| `mashL` | \"left\": left foot/arm, push, pass, tuck (diving), in-turn | □ and L1 | X and LB | **J** | — |\n126\t| `mashR` | \"right\": right foot/arm, push, check, pike (diving), out-turn | ○ and R1 | B and RB | **K** | — |\n127\t| `action` | jump, throw, release, start, take-off, shoot | ✕ | A | **Space** | Left button (aim events only) |\n128\t| `turbo` | fire the YIKES TURBO when the hype flame is full | △ | Y | **Shift** | — |\n129\t| `alt` | hold: tuck, breathe, hold breath, skate burst | R2 (and L2) | RT (and LT) | **L** | Right button (aim events only) |\n130\t| `pause` | pause menu | Options | Menu | Esc, P | — |\n131\t| `confirm` | menus: choose | ✕ | A | Enter, Space | click |\n132\t| `cancel` | menus: back | ○ | B | Esc, Backspace | — |\n133\t\n134\tRules:\n135\t- **`confirm`/`cancel` deliberately do not include J or K** (the engine's DEFAULT preset has them): a sprinter mashing J/K\n136\t  must never skip a results screen. Every screen entered after play has a **0.6 s input lockout** and calls\n137\t  `input.consumeAll()` on enter.\n138\t- **Mash = alternate `mashL` and `mashR`.** PC players mash J/K with the right hand; pad players rock □/○ with a thumb\n139\t  or alternate L1/R1 with index fingers (both pairs are bound). Mash assist (§2.5) offers one-button and hold modes.\n140\t- **Mouse:** menus everywhere (hover moves the focus, click picks; `E.Menu` does this, our widgets copy it). In-world\n141\t  only where the genre uses it: Archery (pointer aims, hold left to draw/release to shoot, hold right to hold breath),\n142\t  Curling (pointer aims the broom target, left button = `action`), Hockey (left: hold/release shoot toward the\n143\t  cursor, right: pass toward the cursor), plus clicking flags on Nation Select and tiles on Single Event. Those event\n144\t  scenes set their own `input` preset that adds `Mouse0`/`Mouse2`; other scenes leave the mouse unbound in play.\n145\t- **Pause and the hub:** in an AI Hub session, \"Create + Options\" belongs to the hub: while pad button 8 (Create) is\n146\t  held, suppress `pause` *before* the engine reads it (needs the exported input hook, §4.6 change 1).\n147\t- Two players on one keyboard: the engine's `game.allowJoin` splits it (P1 WASD + F G H R T Y, P2 arrows + `.` `/` `,`\n148\t  and numpad); our actions map onto the halves in preset order (`mashL, mashR, action, turbo, alt`). Prompts follow\n149\t  automatically because every prompt comes from `input.label(action)`.\n150\t\n151\t### 2.2 Per-event use of the actions\n152\t\n153\t| Event | stick / WASD | mashL □ J | mashR ○ K | action ✕ Space | turbo △ Shift | alt R2 L | mouse |\n154\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n155\t| 100m Dash | — | left foot | right foot | react at the gun; dip at the line | turbo | — | — |\n156\t| Javelin | — | run | run | hold: angle rises; release: throw | turbo | — | — |\n157\t| 50m Freestyle | — | left arm (on the beat) | right arm (on the beat) | start dive, flip turn, touch | turbo | breathe | — |\n158\t| 10m Diving | tap ←/→: half twist | hold: tuck | hold: pike | take-off timing | turbo | — | — |\n159\t| Archery | aim | — | — | hold: draw; release: shoot | turbo | hold: hold breath | pointer aims, L draw, R breath |\n160\t| Weightlifting | ←/→ balance | lift | lift | jerk timing | turbo | — | — |\n161\t| Ski Jump | ↑/↓ balance, body angle | — | — | push off, take-off, telemark | turbo | — | — |\n162\t| Downhill | steer | — | — | pre-jump | turbo | hold: tuck | — |\n163\t| Bobsled | steer | push | push | jump in (load) | turbo | — | — |\n164\t| Figure Skating | ← ↓ ↑ → notes | — | — | hold/release jump notes | turbo | — | — |\n165\t| Curling | aim | in-turn; sweep | out-turn; sweep | start slide; release | turbo | — | pointer aims, L = action |\n166\t| Ice Hockey | skate | pass | check / switch skater | hold: charge, release: shoot | ON FIRE | skate burst | L shoot at cursor, R pass at cursor |\n167\t\n168\t### 2.3 Button prompts and glyphs (a must-build: the pixel font has no ✕ ○ □ △)\n169\t\n170\t`input.label(action)` already returns the right *text* for the player's last device and bindings ('J', 'Space',\n171\t'Left click', '✕', 'A'...), and the engine's `glyphs` setting picks the controller family. But the engine's 5×7 pixel\n172\tfont has no ✕ ○ □ △ (they render as `?`). Build **`O.Prompt`** (src/02-prompts.js):\n173\t\n174\t- `O.Prompt.draw(g, x, y, action, { input = player's input, size: 1|2, label: 'RUN', align })` resolves the code\n175\t  for that player's `lastDevice` from `input.map`/`input.bindings`, then draws an **icon**, never plain text:\n176\t  - PlayStation face buttons: a 9×9 (size 1) / 17×17 (size 2) dark disc with a light rim and the symbol drawn in pixels\n177\t    in its traditional color (✕ blue `#7fb2ff`, ○ red `#ff6b6b`, □ pink `#ff8fd8`, △ green `#4fe0a8`).\n178\t  - Xbox face buttons: disc with the letter (A green `#5bc236`, B red `#e8443a`, X blue `#3a7cf0`, Y yellow `#f2c21b`).\n179\t  - Shoulders/triggers: rounded tab \"L1/R1/L2/R2\" or \"LB/RB/LT/RT\"; Options/Menu: small pill; sticks: a ring with an\n180\t    arrow; d-pad: a cross with the pressed arm lit.\n181\t  - Keyboard: a keycap (rounded rect, 1 px darker bottom edge) with the key's label; Space and Shift are wide caps.\n182\t  - Mouse: a mouse outline with the left/right half filled.\n183\t  - A pair prompt for mashing (`O.Prompt.pair(g, x, y, 'mashL', 'mashR')`) alternates highlight at the player's\n184\t    current mash rhythm (teaches the alternation).\n185\t- Prompts follow the **last input used, per player** (`input.lastDevice`); in multiplayer each lane/turn shows its\n186\t  own player's glyphs.\n187\t- **Default glyph family for controllers: PlayStation.** On first boot, if `game.settings.glyphs` is `'auto'` (never\n188\t  chosen) set it to `'playstation'` (engine change 4). The Settings → Game screen has **Controller prompts:\n189\t  PlayStation / Xbox** (key `promptStyle`) whose `apply` calls `game.settings.set('glyphs', v)`.\n190\t- Also add pixel glyphs for ✕ ○ □ △ to the engine's 5×7 and 3×5 fonts (engine change 3) so any stray label text\n191\t  still reads correctly.\n192\t\n193\t### 2.4 Menus\n194\t\n195\tStick/d-pad/WASD/arrows move the focus (with `input.repeat`); ✕/Enter/Space/click choose; ○/Esc/Backspace go back;\n196\tL1/R1 or Q/E switch tabs (also clickable); hovering with the mouse moves the focus. Every screen shows its prompt strip\n197\tat the bottom (`✕ Select  ○ Back`) with glyph icons.\n198\t\n199\t### 2.5 The game's settings (Settings → Game group, via `new E.Game({ menu: { settings: [...] } })`)\n200\t\n201\t| Key | Label | Options (default **bold**) |\n202\t|---|---|---|\n203\t| `difficulty` | Difficulty | Easy / **Normal** / Hard (CPU skill offset −0.12 / 0 / +0.06; timing windows ×1.25 / 1 / 0.85; qualifying standards per event) |\n204\t| `promptStyle` | Controller prompts | **PlayStation** / Xbox |\n205\t| `mashAssist` | Mash assist | **Off** / One button (any mash button counts as an alternation) / Hold (holding either = steady 72% pace) |\n206\t| `timingAssist` | Timing assist | **Off** / On (all timing windows ×1.4) |\n207\t| `commentary` | Commentary | **Voices + subtitles** / Subtitles only / Off |\n208\t| `textSize` | Text size | **Large** / Huge (×1.5 for subtitles, cards and HUD labels) |\n209\t| `hints` | Hints | **On** / Off (contextual tips, e.g. \"Alternate □ and ○!\") |\n210\t| `voices` | Voices volume (Sound group) | slider 0–1, **0.8** (engine change 6) |\n211\t| `crowd` | Crowd volume (Sound group) | slider 0–1, **0.7** |\n212\t\n213\tThe engine's own Display/Sound/Comfort/Controller/Touch settings, rebinding and full screen stay as they are\n214\t(TASK.md: don't rebuild standard features).\n215\t\n216\t---\n217\t\n218\t## 3. UI and UX (10-foot)\n219\t\n220\t### 3.1 Rules\n221\t\n222\t- **Base resolution** `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true` (a 16:9 TV shows ~427×240 game pixels, scaled ×4.5 at 1080p).\n223\t  Primary text uses `E.font` at **scale 2** (≈63 px tall on a 1080p TV), secondary at scale 1 (≈31 px); never the\n224\t  `tiny` font for anything a player must read. Titles use `E.font.title` scale 4–5.\n225\t- **At most 7 items per list; at most 8 rows per table** (8 nations / 8 lanes). No scrolling tables, no web-style\n226\t  dense screens; long content is paged (L1/R1, ←/→ or clickable page dots).\n227\t- **Focus is loud:** the focused item has a gold bar, a ▸ cursor, a 1 px glow and a gentle scale pulse; a `select`\n228\t  blip on move, `confirm` on choose.\n229\t- **TV-safe margins:** keep HUD and text ≥ 12 game px from every edge; keep the top-right clear of\n230\t  `game.cornerArea()` (the engine's menu/full-screen buttons).\n231\t- **Anchoring:** every HUD element anchors to `r.W`/`r.H`; wide screens show more venue (more stands, more slope).\n232\t- **Information never in color alone:** players are P1–P4 tags plus color (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`), medals carry 1/2/3.\n233\t- **The engine's HTML screens** (pause, settings, controls) must be TV-readable: inject a stylesheet (no engine change)\n234\t  after the engine creates its shell, e.g. `.esh{font-size:clamp(16px,1.55vw,30px)} .esh button{font-size:1.15em;min-height:2.9em;padding:.7em 1em}\n235\t  .esh h2{font-size:2.2em} .esh-mod{width:min(820px,94vw)} .esh input[type=range]{width:min(320px,30vw);height:2.2em}`\n236\t  and theme it gold-on-navy via `E.Shell` theme variables (`accent: '#f2b632'`, `paper: 'rgba(12,16,40,.92)'`).\n237\t  Check with a 1920×1080 screenshot.\n238\t\n239\t### 3.2 Screens (all pixel art unless marked HTML)\n240\t\n241\t| # | Screen | Layout essentials |\n242\t|---|---|---|\n243\t| 1 | Boot (engine) | `boot: { title: 'OLYMPIYIKES!', steps: [...], messages: ['Inflating the blimp', 'Waxing the skis', 'Teaching the crowd to wave', 'Calming the mascot'] }` |\n244\t| 2 | Title | stadium at sunset (summer half) beside a floodlit ski jump (winter half), cauldron burning, blimp, the 8 athletes warming up in a row, logo `OLYMPIYIKES!` (E.font.title scale 5, gold→orange, red outline, depth 3), emblem, tagline, pulsing \"PRESS ✕\" (glyph by device), engine version tiny bottom-right. Idle 10 s → attract |\n245\t| 3 | Main menu | left: big list (§1.5); right: a live vignette for the focused item (parade / pictogram wheel / trophy / the mascot); bottom: description + prompt strip |\n246\t| 4 | Touch-only notice | replaces \"PRESS ✕\": panel **\"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\"**, one line \"Connect a controller or keyboard to play.\", a big **SETTINGS** button (tap → `game.menu('settings')`). Attract still runs |\n247\t| 5 | Games setup | rows: Length (Full 12 / Quick 4 + playlist), Difficulty, Qualifying (On for 1P / Off), START |\n248\t| 6 | Nation select + join | 4×2 grid of big flags (48×32) with codes; each player's cursor in its color with a P-tag; right panel: the focused nation's athlete (live rig cheering), name, 3 specialty pictograms, one-line bio; \"P2–P4: press ✕ on another controller, or 2 on the keyboard\" |\n249\t| 7 | Opening ceremony | cinematic (§7.4), hold ✕ for 1 s to skip |\n250\t| 8 | Day card (\"broadcast bumper\") | `DAY 3 · 50m FREESTYLE` / venue / weather; lower third: **UP CLOSE & PERSONAL** with a big portrait and 2–3 subtitle lines; optional rival line |\n251\t| 9 | Event card | pictogram + name; GOAL line; 3–4 rows of glyph icon(s) + verb; Qualifying standard, World record; prompts `✕ Start  ○ Back` (+ `△ Practice` in Single Event) |\n252\t| 10 | Event HUD | per event (§6); common: top-left event + pictogram, top-right clock/score (big digits), bottom: per-player prompt strip and the hype flame |\n253\t| 11 | Turn card | \"P2 — YOUR TURN\" in player color / \"ROUND 2 OF 3\" + top-4 standings |\n254\t| 12 | Results | ≤ 8 rows: rank, medal icon, flag, athlete, performance, notes (WR, GR, PB, Q, DNF, DQ); humans' rows in player color; records burst; `✕ Continue` |\n255\t| 13 | Initials entry | `E.Arcade` letter wheel when a human makes a top-5 table (skipped in hub) |\n256\t| 14 | Medal ceremony | podium in the venue, medals, three flags rising, the winner's procedural anthem, fireworks |\n257\t| 15 | Medal table | 8 nation rows (flag, code, G S B, total), yours highlighted; a ticker of the day's highlights |\n258\t| 16 | Story beat | day-card layout with one or two portraits (halftime report, incidents, rival) |\n259\t| 17 | Torch lost / Games over | \"YIKES! NO QUALIFYING MARK — TORCHES ●●○\" → retry; at 0: \"GAMES OVER — CONTINUE? 9\" |\n260\t| 18 | Closing + ending + credits | cinematic, ending text by rank, credits roll (paged, skippable) |\n261\t| 19 | Single Event select | 4×3 grid of event tiles (pictogram, name, season color, your best medal); bottom: description |\n262\t| 20 | Records | tabs per event (←/→ or L1/R1): top-5 rows; tabs LAURELS (12 tiles) and CABINET (nation × event medals as icons) |\n263\t| 21 | How to play | Basics pages + list of 12 event cards (read-only) |\n264\t| 22 | Settings / Controls / Pause | engine HTML (scaled, themed) |\n265\t| 23 | About & Credits | paged: Source Information · Testing Feedback · Engine Feedback · Prompt Feedback · Credits & engine changes · Controls |\n266\t| 24 | Dev panel | pixel overlay (§13) |\n267\t| 25 | Hub card / hub splash | §12.2 |\n268\t\n269\tTransitions: `game.go(scene, data, { fade: 'wipe' })` between menu screens, `'iris'` into events, `'white'` into\n270\tceremonies (reduce-motion turns them into plain fades by itself).\n271\t\n272\t### 3.3 Touch-only devices\n273\t\n274\t`touchOnly = matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches\n275\t&& navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0` **and** no key press or gamepad has been seen this visit. While true: show screen 4,\n276\thide engine touch buttons (scenes declare `touch: []`; set the engine's `touch` setting to `'off'` at boot),\n277\tlet the attract mode run after 10 s; a tap during attract returns to the notice. A `gamepadconnected` event or any key\n278\tpress flips `touchOnly` to false at once and shows the normal title.\n279\t\n280\t---\n281\t\n282\t## 4. Architecture\n283\t\n284\t### 4.1 Tech stack\n285\t\n286\t- **Engine:** our copy of the my-3D2dge v0.85.2 full engine (`engine/my-3d2dge.js`: the core plus every module; we use\n287\t  the `road`, `rhythm`, `physics`, `arcade` and `songs` modules from it). Plain JavaScript, no libraries, no CDN, no\n288\t  network at runtime.\n289\t- **Online:** AI Hub `session.js` 1.4.0, unmodified, first script in `<head>`.\n290\t- **Tooling:** Node (headless tests via `vm`, as `game/test.mjs` does), Playwright 1.63.0 + Chromium (browser checks,\n291\t  hub harness, screenshots), the handoff's `tools/check.mjs` and `tools/filmstrip.mjs`.\n292\t- **Deliverable:** one self-contained HTML (engine + session client + game inlined), ~1.8 MB, works from `file://`.\n293\t\n294\t### 4.2 Folder layout\n295\t\n296\t```\n297\tOlympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174837/\n298\t  index.html                 dev page: same <head> as the bundle; loads engine + src files in order (the list is the bundle's source of truth)\n299\t  engine/my-3d2dge.js        the engine + our changes, each marked  // OLY: <why>\n300\t  vendor/hub/                session.js (bundled, unmodified) + harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md (tests only)\n301\t  vendor/my-3d2dge/          sparse clone of the handoff: docs, kits, tools (reference + Playwright); never bundled\n302\t  src/                       the game: one IIFE per file, all attach to window.OLY (alias O)\n303\t  tests/                     run.mjs (the one command) + suites\n304\t  tools/bundle.mjs           builds the root HTML from index.html's script list\n305\t  tools/shots.mjs            screenshot/filmstrip helper around the handoff tools\n306\t```\n307\t\n308\t### 4.3 Source files (load order = bundle order)\n309\t\n310\t| File | Responsibility |\n311\t|---|---|\n312\t| `00-core.js` | creates `window.OLY`, defines `OLY_KEYS` and `GAME_SETTINGS`, then the `E.Game` (§4.4), constants (`M`, palettes), `O.def(kind, id, spec)` registries, `O.bus` (on/emit), `O.store` (E.store wrappers), `O.rng(label)` streams (§4.7), formatting helpers |\n313\t| `01-input.js` | per-scene presets (archery/curling/hockey add mouse), **pilots** (§5.2), `Mash` meter, `O.AutoPlayer` (bot intents → real `input.tap/hold`), idle/any-input tracker, touch-only detection, hub Create+Options guard |\n314\t| `02-prompts.js` | `O.Prompt` glyph renderer (§2.3) |\n315\t| `03-ui.js` | 10-foot widgets: `Menu10` (vertical list), `Grid10` (tile grid), `Tabs`, `Panel`, `Banner` (big stamped words), `Ticker`, `LowerThird`, `Toast`, page dots, number/time formatters; mouse hover/click via `input.pointer()`/`clicked()` |\n316\t| `04-audio.js` | SFX definitions (`audio.define`), songs (§9), `O.Voice` gibberish synth, `O.CrowdAudio`, `O.Anthem` generator, stings |\n317\t| `05-art.js` | palettes per venue, flag painter, pictograms, emblem, equipment sprites, stands + crowd painter, jumbotron (dot matrix), podium, fireworks/confetti helpers, mascot (Blob) |\n318\t| `06-pose.js` | `O.Pose` library and `O.Gear` (equipment drawn at joints), body rotation (§8.2) |\n319\t| `07-athletes.js` | `NATIONS` data (§7.1), teammates, `O.makeRig(nation, sport, opts)` outfits per sport, voice params |\n320\t| `08-commentary.js` | `O.Comms`: commentators, line banks (§7.6), cue rules, subtitles + voice + portraits |\n321\t| `09-hype.js` | `O.Hype` (meter, turbo), `O.Yikes` (stamp, count, sympathy hype), `O.Crowd` (visual excitement), slow-mo/photo helpers |\n322\t| `10-records.js` | world records seed table, games records, personal bests, top-5 tables via `E.Arcade`, points formulas, medal table, party points, laurels, cabinet |\n323\t| `11-ghosts.js` | ghost recording/playback (local turns + hub), compact samples |\n324\t| `12-eventkit.js` | the event contract, `O.defEvent`, the phase runner, formats (race / attempts / match / program), CPU simulation, results assembly, qualifying, timing widgets (`TimingBar`, `Ring`, `Balance`, `Sway`, `StrokeBeat`) |\n325\t| `20-ev-sprint.js` … `35-ev-hockey.js` | one file per event (§6), ids: `sprint javelin swim diving archery weights skijump downhill bobsled figure curling hockey` |\n326\t| `40-sc-title.js` | title, main menu, touch-only notice, attract director (§11) |\n327\t| `41-sc-games.js` | games setup, nation select/join, opening ceremony, day card, story beats, event card, turn card, results, initials, medal ceremony, medal table, torch/continue, closing + ending + credits |\n328\t| `42-sc-misc.js` | single event select, records, how to play, about & credits, pose lab |\n329\t| `43-dev.js` | dev panel, tuning sliders, cheats, overlays (§13) |\n330\t| `44-hub.js` | AI Hub session flow (§12.2) |\n331\t| `99-main.js` | registers scenes, deep links (`#ev/<id>`, `#scene/<name>`, `#attract`, `#poselab`, `#games/day/<n>`), `game.debug`, `window.__oly` for tests, `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot })` |\n332\t\n333\tEach file: `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge, O = OLY; ... })();`. Tests load exactly the same files in the same\n334\torder into a `vm` context (read the list from `index.html` so it can't drift).\n335\t\n336\t### 4.4 The game object\n337\t\n338\t```js\n339\tconst game = O.game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'brawler', input: OLY_KEYS,\n340\t  id: 'olympiyikes', bg: '#0b0f24',\n341\t  menu: { items: inHub ? [] : [['Restart event', () => O.restartEvent()], ['How to play', () => O.showEventCard()], ['Dev mode', () => O.Dev.open()]],\n342\t          settings: GAME_SETTINGS /* §2.5 */, quit: inHub ? false : 'title',\n343\t          controls: { mashL: 'Left (mash, pass, tuck)', mashR: 'Right (mash, check, pike)', action: 'Action (jump, throw, release)',\n344\t                      turbo: 'Yikes Turbo', alt: 'Hold (tuck, breathe, steady)' } } });\n345\t```\n346\t`inHub = !!(window.AIHub && AIHub.active)` (true from the client's first line). `OLY_KEYS` and `GAME_SETTINGS` are defined\n347\tin `00-core.js` above this call (the later files only add to them); the menu callbacks are arrows because their targets\n348\tare defined in later files. Menu items are static, so each item's\n349\tfunction is context-aware: outside an event \"Restart event\" and \"How to play\" show `game.note('Not in an event')`.\n350\t\n351\t### 4.5 Scenes\n352\t\n353\t`O.scene(name, def)` registers a wrapped engine scene. The wrapper: calls `input.consumeAll()` on enter; applies the\n354\t0.6 s lockout (`def.lock`); if the dev panel is open, runs `O.Dev.update()` instead of `def.update` and draws the panel\n355\tafter `def.draw`; draws global overlays (toasts, commentary lower third, attract banner, \"YIKES\" stamps, laurel toasts)\n356\tafter the scene; tracks idle time on menu scenes (`def.menu: true`) for the attract mode; routes `back()`.\n357\t\n358\tScenes: `title`, `menu`, `touchonly` (a state of `title`), `attract`, `gamesSetup`, `nations`, `opening`, `dayCard`,\n359\t`beat`, `eventCard`, `turnCard` (overlay state inside events), `ev:<id>` ×12, `results`, `initials`, `podium`,\n360\t`medals`, `torch`, `closing`, `credits`, `single`, `records`, `howto`, `about`, `poselab`, `hubCard`, `hubSplash`.\n361\t\n362\t`O.flow` is the state machine above them: it holds the current **meet** (§4.8) and decides the next scene after each\n363\tone ends (`O.flow.next(from, payload)`), for each mode (games, quick, single, hub, attract).\n364\t\n365\t### 4.6 Engine changes (all marked `// OLY:` in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`; list them on the About screen)\n366\t\n367\t1. **Export the hooks:** `E.HOOKS = HOOKS;` so the game can run code at the start of every step even while paused\n368\t   (`HOOKS.input`): hub Create+Options guard, global any-input detection.\n369\t2. **Cosmetic randomness off `Math.random`:** the Humanoid (`this.t` start phase, blink timer), `Particles` and\n370\t   `hitFx`'s number jitter draw from a module-level `const cosmetic = rng(0xC05E7)` instead of `Math.random`. Then\n371\t   cosmetics never shift gameplay randomness, the hub card draws no `Math.random` numbers, and tests stay\n372\t   deterministic.\n373\t3. **Font glyphs:** add ✕ ○ □ △ to the 5×7 (`G57`) and 3×5 (`GLYPHS`) tables.\n374\t4. **PlayStation by default:** the `glyphs` setting's default becomes `'playstation'`.\n375\t5. **Pose hook on the Humanoid:** at the end of `solvePose()`, if `this.post` is a function call `this.post(this, this.J)`;\n376\t   then, if `this.bodyPitch || this.bodyRoll`, rotate every joint (and cape/hair points, as `_roll` does) about\n377\t   `this.pivot` (default: hip centre) by pitch (forward–up plane) and roll (right–up plane), **without** `_roll`'s\n378\t   floor lift. This is what flips, dives, swims, tumbles and V-style flight need (§8.2).\n379\t6. **Voice bus:** `ChipAudio` gets a `_voiceBus` gain under the master, a `voiceVolume` field honored by\n380\t   `applyVolume()`, and the settings get `voices` (Sound group, default .8). `O.Voice` connects to it.\n381\t\n382\t### 4.7 Randomness and determinism\n383\t\n384\t- **Gameplay randomness only through labeled streams:** `O.rng(label)` returns a `[0,1)` generator. In a hub session\n385\t  it is `AIHub.rng(label)` (identical on every copy); otherwise `E.rng(hash(meet.seed + '|' + label))`. Labels are\n386\t  namespaced: `'sprint:gun'`, `'javelin:wind:r2'`, `'cpu:FRM:skijump:j1'`, `'judges:diving:SUN:d3'`.\n387\t- Cosmetics (particles, blinks, crowd bob) never touch `Math.random` (engine change 2) or the gameplay streams.\n388\t- `meet.seed` comes from the clock when a meet starts; tests and the dev panel can set it.\n389\t- Every event sim is a function of (state, intents, streams), independent of drawing, so a sim can run headless,\n390\t  twice, and hash the same (the kits' \"proof\" idea).\n391\t\n392\t### 4.8 Data model\n393\t\n394\t```\n395\tNation   { id: 'SUN', name: 'SUNVALE', colors: { a, b, c }, flag: { kind, ... }, athlete: { name, rig, bio, voice },\n396\t           team: [3 names], skills: { sprint: .92, ... }, anthem: { seed, key, bpm } }\n397\tAthlete  { id, nation, name, rig, gear, pilot, lane, human, player (0-3 | null), hype, turboT, state: {}, attempts: [],\n398\t           best, notes: [], ghost }\n399\tPilot    { kind: 'human' | 'bot' | 'ghost' | 'net', input?, skill, cheat?: 'perfect' | null }\n400\tIntent   { L, R, A, T (pressed this step), Lh, Rh, Ad, Au, X (held / released), mv: [x, y], aim: [x, y] | null }"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7cbd2ff4-8848-4b45-ab1b-b913e7b74016","uuid":"5f4722ce-e392-4d83-b0f2-dec05bc0dc5e","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:51:03.307Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261104-075058__x0e4b02/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Olympiyikes!\n\n**Tagline:** *Faster. Higher. Yikes.*\n**One line:** the complete Summer *and* Winter Games, held in the same fortnight in one twin-city host, played as\nan over-the-top arcade multi-event: 12 events, 8 nations, ceremonies, a rival, two hyperventilating commentators,\n1–4 players on the couch and AI Hub ghost heats online.\n\n> **How the title was picked (TASK.md rule):** 50 riffs on \"Olympic Games\" were brainstormed silently, then\n> `secrets.randbelow(50)` (Python, OS CSPRNG) returned **16 → \"Olympiyikes!\"**. Do not re-roll it. Its file-name\n> form is `Olympiyikes`; the SOURCE token is `OlympicGames`.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Read this first (ground rules for the builders)\n\n- **Inputs:** `TASK.md` (requirements) + this plan. If they disagree, TASK.md wins. Where this plan is silent, follow\n  the engine's own docs (`API.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`) and the plan's spirit. Everything listed here ships: **no TODOs,\n  placeholders, locked \"coming soon\" slots or stubbed features.**\n- **Names (with this TASK.md):** dev folder `Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174837/`, deliverable\n  `Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174837.html` in the root (beside TASK.md), and\n  `exit-interview.json` in the root. If your TASK.md's name pattern carries a different model/date, use your pattern\n  with `Olympiyikes` and `OlympicGames`.\n- **Shared machine:** read only the files you need (other agents share the parent directories, ports and processes).\n  Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), run\n  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from your dev folder, read the port it prints, and confirm the served page\n  is *your* game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by PID. Never `pkill`, `killall`,\n  `fuser -k`. During planning `/tmp` was 100% full: if npm/Playwright fail with ENOSPC, set `TMPDIR=<dev>/.tmp`.\n- **Engine:** my-3D2dge handoff package, **v0.85.2** at planning time\n  (`https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`). We own our copy and may change it (§4.6).\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client **1.4.0** (`https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`).\n- **No subagents/workflows to debate, review, judge or audit** (TASK.md). Tests are scripts.\n- **Units used in this plan:** world units (u) are the engine's (one tile = 16 u, z is up). Distances shown to the\n  player are meters; each event says its scale (default **M = 12 u per meter**). Times in seconds unless marked ms.\n  The engine runs fixed steps of 1/120 s.\n\n### 0.1 Fetch what you build on (once, inside the dev folder)\n\n```sh\nD=Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174837 && mkdir -p $D/{engine,src,tests,tools,vendor/hub,.tmp} && cd $D\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git vendor/my-3d2dge\ngit -C vendor/my-3d2dge sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\ncp vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js engine/my-3d2dge.js          # our engine copy (we edit this one)\nfor f in session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js README.md; do\n  curl -fsSL -o vendor/hub/$f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\n(cd vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff && export TMPDIR=\"$(cd ../../../.. && pwd)/.tmp\" && npm install && npx playwright install chromium)\n```\n\nRead whole: `vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/API.md`, `README.md`, `kits/README.md`, `docs/ASSETS.md`,\n`vendor/hub/README.md`. Read as references (copy patterns, not whole files): `kits/sports.js` (hockey), `kits/road.js`\n(downhill/bobsled), `kits/rhythm.js` (figure skating), `kits/pinball.js` + `modules/physics.js` (curling),\n`kits/kart.js` (drawing a rig through a custom view), `modules/arcade.js` (record tables, initials), `game/test.mjs`\nand `kits/test.mjs` (headless harness). Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names; don't read it whole.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n### 1.1 Pitch\n\nFor the first time in history the Summer and Winter Games share one fortnight and one host: **Mount Glory–Sunny Bay**,\nwhere a ski jump towers over the beach and the swimming pool shares a wall with the ice rink. Eight fictional nations\nsend their stars. You pick a nation and its athlete, light the cauldron, and chase gold across **12 events** that\nalternate summer and winter, while defending champion **Max Overdrive of Turbonia** insists, loudly, that it is all\npointless because he will win. Everything is broadcast like the real thing (parade of nations, \"Up Close & Personal\"\nprofiles, photo finishes, medal ceremonies with anthems, a medal table) and played like an arcade cabinet (mashing,\ntiming, aiming, steering, rhythm, physics; a HYPE meter that sets you on fire; world records that explode into\nfireworks). When it goes wrong, it goes *gloriously* wrong: false starts, belly flops, ski-jump tumbles, bobsleds on\ntheir roof. That's an **OLYMPIYIKES!**, and the game celebrates it.\n\n### 1.2 Pillars\n\n1. **Ten seconds to learn, a lifetime to perfect.** Each event teaches one verb (mash, time, aim, steer, keep the beat,\n   sweep) on one card, and has a perfect-play ceiling with world records to chase.\n2. **Broadcast spectacle.** Ceremonies, commentators, crowd, jumbotron, slow-mo, photo finishes, anthems, records.\n3. **Failure is a show.** Every event has at least one comic \"Yikes\" moment that is funny, fast to recover from, and\n   even pays a little hype (§5.8).\n4. **Both seasons.** 6 summer + 6 winter events on alternating days; each venue has its own look, light and music.\n5. **Couch first, online too.** 1–4 local players in every mode; AI Hub ghost heats when the hub launches the game.\n\n### 1.3 Genre and perspective (decisions)\n\n- **Genre:** arcade multi-event sports (Track & Field / Winter Games lineage) with a short campaign, local party\n  play and online ghost heats. Each event is a compact mini-game built on one shared event framework.\n- **Perspective:** per event, all on the engine's native 2D views (pixel art at `res: 'ps1'`, `expand: true`):\n  - **2.5D side-on lanes** (`'brawler'` view, pitch 25): 100m Dash, Javelin, 50m Freestyle, Weightlifting.\n  - **Pure side view** (`'side'`): 10m Diving, Ski Jump, the archer's inset.\n  - **Target close-up overlay:** Archery's main view.\n  - **Pseudo-3D behind the athlete** (the `road` module, `E.Road`): Downhill, Bobsled.\n  - **Three-quarter** (`'threequarter'`): Figure Skating, Ice Hockey.\n  - Ceremonies, podiums and the title use `'brawler'` in their venue.\n  - **Top-down** (`'topdown'`): Curling.\n  Rationale: each event gets the camera its classic arcade ancestors used, and the engine has a native, tested view\n  for every one of them.\n\n### 1.4 Core loop\n\nPick nation → (ceremony) → **day card** (who, where, a human-interest segment) → **event card** (one screen: goal and\ncontrols with button glyphs) → **compete** (live vs CPU rivals and friends) → **results** (records, qualifying) →\n**medal ceremony** → **medal table** (story beat) → next day. 12 days, then the closing ceremony and an ending that\ndepends on your nation's rank.\n\n### 1.5 Modes (main menu, in this order)\n\n| Item | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| CONTINUE GAMES | only when an autosave exists: resumes The Games at the next day |\n| THE GAMES | the campaign: opening ceremony, 12 days (all 12 events), story beats, halftime report, closing ceremony, ending; or, chosen on its setup screen, **Quick Games**: 4 events (playlists *Track & Pool*, *Snow & Ice*, *Greatest Hits*, *Random 4*) with mini ceremonies and party points. 1–4 players |\n| SINGLE EVENT | any of the 12 events, vs CPU or practice (no rivals); 1–4 players |\n| RECORDS | world records and top-5 tables per event (initials), the Laurel cabinet, the Medal cabinet |\n| HOW TO PLAY | Basics (mash, timing, hype & turbo, yikes) + every event's card |\n| SETTINGS | the engine's settings screen plus the game's own (§2.5) |\n| ABOUT & CREDITS | Source Information, Testing/Engine/Prompt Feedback, credits, engine changes, controls |\n\nOnline play is not a menu item: it starts only when the AI Hub launches the page (§12.2).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Controls and input (controller-first, keyboard and mouse native)\n\n### 2.1 The action map (custom preset `OLY_KEYS`, replaces the engine presets for the whole game)\n\n| Action | Meaning | DualSense (default glyphs) | Xbox glyphs (setting) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `up/down/left/right` | move, steer, aim, balance, menu focus | left stick, d-pad | left stick, d-pad | WASD, arrows | — |\n| `mashL` | \"left\": left foot/arm, push, pass, tuck (diving), in-turn | □ and L1 | X and LB | **J** | — |\n| `mashR` | \"right\": right foot/arm, push, check, pike (diving), out-turn | ○ and R1 | B and RB | **K** | — |\n| `action` | jump, throw, release, start, take-off, shoot | ✕ | A | **Space** | Left button (aim events only) |\n| `turbo` | fire the YIKES TURBO when the hype flame is full | △ | Y | **Shift** | — |\n| `alt` | hold: tuck, breathe, hold breath, skate burst | R2 (and L2) | RT (and LT) | **L** | Right button (aim events only) |\n| `pause` | pause menu | Options | Menu | Esc, P | — |\n| `confirm` | menus: choose | ✕ | A | Enter, Space | click |\n| `cancel` | menus: back | ○ | B | Esc, Backspace | — |\n\nRules:\n- **`confirm`/`cancel` deliberately do not include J or K** (the engine's DEFAULT preset has them): a sprinter mashing J/K\n  must never skip a results screen. Every screen entered after play has a **0.6 s input lockout** and calls\n  `input.consumeAll()` on enter.\n- **Mash = alternate `mashL` and `mashR`.** PC players mash J/K with the right hand; pad players rock □/○ with a thumb\n  or alternate L1/R1 with index fingers (both pairs are bound). Mash assist (§2.5) offers one-button and hold modes.\n- **Mouse:** menus everywhere (hover moves the focus, click picks; `E.Menu` does this, our widgets copy it). In-world\n  only where the genre uses it: Archery (pointer aims, hold left to draw/release to shoot, hold right to hold breath),\n  Curling (pointer aims the broom target, left button = `action`), Hockey (left: hold/release shoot toward the\n  cursor, right: pass toward the cursor), plus clicking flags on Nation Select and tiles on Single Event. Those event\n  scenes set their own `input` preset that adds `Mouse0`/`Mouse2`; other scenes leave the mouse unbound in play.\n- **Pause and the hub:** in an AI Hub session, \"Create + Options\" belongs to the hub: while pad button 8 (Create) is\n  held, suppress `pause` *before* the engine reads it (needs the exported input hook, §4.6 change 1).\n- Two players on one keyboard: the engine's `game.allowJoin` splits it (P1 WASD + F G H R T Y, P2 arrows + `.` `/` `,`\n  and numpad); our actions map onto the halves in preset order (`mashL, mashR, action, turbo, alt`). Prompts follow\n  automatically because every prompt comes from `input.label(action)`.\n\n### 2.2 Per-event use of the actions\n\n| Event | stick / WASD | mashL □ J | mashR ○ K | action ✕ Space | turbo △ Shift | alt R2 L | mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 100m Dash | — | left foot | right foot | react at the gun; dip at the line | turbo | — | — |\n| Javelin | — | run | run | hold: angle rises; release: throw | turbo | — | — |\n| 50m Freestyle | — | left arm (on the beat) | right arm (on the beat) | start dive, flip turn, touch | turbo | breathe | — |\n| 10m Diving | tap ←/→: half twist | hold: tuck | hold: pike | take-off timing | turbo | — | — |\n| Archery | aim | — | — | hold: draw; release: shoot | turbo | hold: hold breath | pointer aims, L draw, R breath |\n| Weightlifting | ←/→ balance | lift | lift | jerk timing | turbo | — | — |\n| Ski Jump | ↑/↓ balance, body angle | — | — | push off, take-off, telemark | turbo | — | — |\n| Downhill | steer | — | — | pre-jump | turbo | hold: tuck | — |\n| Bobsled | steer | push | push | jump in (load) | turbo | — | — |\n| Figure Skating | ← ↓ ↑ → notes | — | — | hold/release jump notes | turbo | — | — |\n| Curling | aim | in-turn; sweep | out-turn; sweep | start slide; release | turbo | — | pointer aims, L = action |\n| Ice Hockey | skate | pass | check / switch skater | hold: charge, release: shoot | ON FIRE | skate burst | L shoot at cursor, R pass at cursor |\n\n### 2.3 Button prompts and glyphs (a must-build: the pixel font has no ✕ ○ □ △)\n\n`input.label(action)` already returns the right *text* for the player's last device and bindings ('J', 'Space',\n'Left click', '✕', 'A'...), and the engine's `glyphs` setting picks the controller family. But the engine's 5×7 pixel\nfont has no ✕ ○ □ △ (they render as `?`). Build **`O.Prompt`** (src/02-prompts.js):\n\n- `O.Prompt.draw(g, x, y, action, { input = player's input, size: 1|2, label: 'RUN', align })` resolves the code\n  for that player's `lastDevice` from `input.map`/`input.bindings`, then draws an **icon**, never plain text:\n  - PlayStation face buttons: a 9×9 (size 1) / 17×17 (size 2) dark disc with a light rim and the symbol drawn in pixels\n    in its traditional color (✕ blue `#7fb2ff`, ○ red `#ff6b6b`, □ pink `#ff8fd8`, △ green `#4fe0a8`).\n  - Xbox face buttons: disc with the letter (A green `#5bc236`, B red `#e8443a`, X blue `#3a7cf0`, Y yellow `#f2c21b`).\n  - Shoulders/triggers: rounded tab \"L1/R1/L2/R2\" or \"LB/RB/LT/RT\"; Options/Menu: small pill; sticks: a ring with an\n    arrow; d-pad: a cross with the pressed arm lit.\n  - Keyboard: a keycap (rounded rect, 1 px darker bottom edge) with the key's label; Space and Shift are wide caps.\n  - Mouse: a mouse outline with the left/right half filled.\n  - A pair prompt for mashing (`O.Prompt.pair(g, x, y, 'mashL', 'mashR')`) alternates highlight at the player's\n    current mash rhythm (teaches the alternation).\n- Prompts follow the **last input used, per player** (`input.lastDevice`); in multiplayer each lane/turn shows its\n  own player's glyphs.\n- **Default glyph family for controllers: PlayStation.** On first boot, if `game.settings.glyphs` is `'auto'` (never\n  chosen) set it to `'playstation'` (engine change 4). The Settings → Game screen has **Controller prompts:\n  PlayStation / Xbox** (key `promptStyle`) whose `apply` calls `game.settings.set('glyphs', v)`.\n- Also add pixel glyphs for ✕ ○ □ △ to the engine's 5×7 and 3×5 fonts (engine change 3) so any stray label text\n  still reads correctly.\n\n### 2.4 Menus\n\nStick/d-pad/WASD/arrows move the focus (with `input.repeat`); ✕/Enter/Space/click choose; ○/Esc/Backspace go back;\nL1/R1 or Q/E switch tabs (also clickable); hovering with the mouse moves the focus. Every screen shows its prompt strip\nat the bottom (`✕ Select  ○ Back`) with glyph icons.\n\n### 2.5 The game's settings (Settings → Game group, via `new E.Game({ menu: { settings: [...] } })`)\n\n| Key | Label | Options (default **bold**) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `difficulty` | Difficulty | Easy / **Normal** / Hard (CPU skill offset −0.12 / 0 / +0.06; timing windows ×1.25 / 1 / 0.85; qualifying standards per event) |\n| `promptStyle` | Controller prompts | **PlayStation** / Xbox |\n| `mashAssist` | Mash assist | **Off** / One button (any mash button counts as an alternation) / Hold (holding either = steady 72% pace) |\n| `timingAssist` | Timing assist | **Off** / On (all timing windows ×1.4) |\n| `commentary` | Commentary | **Voices + subtitles** / Subtitles only / Off |\n| `textSize` | Text size | **Large** / Huge (×1.5 for subtitles, cards and HUD labels) |\n| `hints` | Hints | **On** / Off (contextual tips, e.g. \"Alternate □ and ○!\") |\n| `voices` | Voices volume (Sound group) | slider 0–1, **0.8** (engine change 6) |\n| `crowd` | Crowd volume (Sound group) | slider 0–1, **0.7** |\n\nThe engine's own Display/Sound/Comfort/Controller/Touch settings, rebinding and full screen stay as they are\n(TASK.md: don't rebuild standard features).\n\n---\n\n## 3. UI and UX (10-foot)\n\n### 3.1 Rules\n\n- **Base resolution** `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true` (a 16:9 TV shows ~427×240 game pixels, scaled ×4.5 at 1080p).\n  Primary text uses `E.font` at **scale 2** (≈63 px tall on a 1080p TV), secondary at scale 1 (≈31 px); never the\n  `tiny` font for anything a player must read. Titles use `E.font.title` scale 4–5.\n- **At most 7 items per list; at most 8 rows per table** (8 nations / 8 lanes). No scrolling tables, no web-style\n  dense screens; long content is paged (L1/R1, ←/→ or clickable page dots).\n- **Focus is loud:** the focused item has a gold bar, a ▸ cursor, a 1 px glow and a gentle scale pulse; a `select`\n  blip on move, `confirm` on choose.\n- **TV-safe margins:** keep HUD and text ≥ 12 game px from every edge; keep the top-right clear of\n  `game.cornerArea()` (the engine's menu/full-screen buttons).\n- **Anchoring:** every HUD element anchors to `r.W`/`r.H`; wide screens show more venue (more stands, more slope).\n- **Information never in color alone:** players are P1–P4 tags plus color (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`), medals carry 1/2/3.\n- **The engine's HTML screens** (pause, settings, controls) must be TV-readable: inject a stylesheet (no engine change)\n  after the engine creates its shell, e.g. `.esh{font-size:clamp(16px,1.55vw,30px)} .esh button{font-size:1.15em;min-height:2.9em;padding:.7em 1em}\n  .esh h2{font-size:2.2em} .esh-mod{width:min(820px,94vw)} .esh input[type=range]{width:min(320px,30vw);height:2.2em}`\n  and theme it gold-on-navy via `E.Shell` theme variables (`accent: '#f2b632'`, `paper: 'rgba(12,16,40,.92)'`).\n  Check with a 1920×1080 screenshot.\n\n### 3.2 Screens (all pixel art unless marked HTML)\n\n| # | Screen | Layout essentials |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Boot (engine) | `boot: { title: 'OLYMPIYIKES!', steps: [...], messages: ['Inflating the blimp', 'Waxing the skis', 'Teaching the crowd to wave', 'Calming the mascot'] }` |\n| 2 | Title | stadium at sunset (summer half) beside a floodlit ski jump (winter half), cauldron burning, blimp, the 8 athletes warming up in a row, logo `OLYMPIYIKES!` (E.font.title scale 5, gold→orange, red outline, depth 3), emblem, tagline, pulsing \"PRESS ✕\" (glyph by device), engine version tiny bottom-right. Idle 10 s → attract |\n| 3 | Main menu | left: big list (§1.5); right: a live vignette for the focused item (parade / pictogram wheel / trophy / the mascot); bottom: description + prompt strip |\n| 4 | Touch-only notice | replaces \"PRESS ✕\": panel **\"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\"**, one line \"Connect a controller or keyboard to play.\", a big **SETTINGS** button (tap → `game.menu('settings')`). Attract still runs |\n| 5 | Games setup | rows: Length (Full 12 / Quick 4 + playlist), Difficulty, Qualifying (On for 1P / Off), START |\n| 6 | Nation select + join | 4×2 grid of big flags (48×32) with codes; each player's cursor in its color with a P-tag; right panel: the focused nation's athlete (live rig cheering), name, 3 specialty pictograms, one-line bio; \"P2–P4: press ✕ on another controller, or 2 on the keyboard\" |\n| 7 | Opening ceremony | cinematic (§7.4), hold ✕ for 1 s to skip |\n| 8 | Day card (\"broadcast bumper\") | `DAY 3 · 50m FREESTYLE` / venue / weather; lower third: **UP CLOSE & PERSONAL** with a big portrait and 2–3 subtitle lines; optional rival line |\n| 9 | Event card | pictogram + name; GOAL line; 3–4 rows of glyph icon(s) + verb; Qualifying standard, World record; prompts `✕ Start  ○ Back` (+ `△ Practice` in Single Event) |\n| 10 | Event HUD | per event (§6); common: top-left event + pictogram, top-right clock/score (big digits), bottom: per-player prompt strip and the hype flame |\n| 11 | Turn card | \"P2 — YOUR TURN\" in player color / \"ROUND 2 OF 3\" + top-4 standings |\n| 12 | Results | ≤ 8 rows: rank, medal icon, flag, athlete, performance, notes (WR, GR, PB, Q, DNF, DQ); humans' rows in player color; records burst; `✕ Continue` |\n| 13 | Initials entry | `E.Arcade` letter wheel when a human makes a top-5 table (skipped in hub) |\n| 14 | Medal ceremony | podium in the venue, medals, three flags rising, the winner's procedural anthem, fireworks |\n| 15 | Medal table | 8 nation rows (flag, code, G S B, total), yours highlighted; a ticker of the day's highlights |\n| 16 | Story beat | day-card layout with one or two portraits (halftime report, incidents, rival) |\n| 17 | Torch lost / Games over | \"YIKES! NO QUALIFYING MARK — TORCHES ●●○\" → retry; at 0: \"GAMES OVER — CONTINUE? 9\" |\n| 18 | Closing + ending + credits | cinematic, ending text by rank, credits roll (paged, skippable) |\n| 19 | Single Event select | 4×3 grid of event tiles (pictogram, name, season color, your best medal); bottom: description |\n| 20 | Records | tabs per event (←/→ or L1/R1): top-5 rows; tabs LAURELS (12 tiles) and CABINET (nation × event medals as icons) |\n| 21 | How to play | Basics pages + list of 12 event cards (read-only) |\n| 22 | Settings / Controls / Pause | engine HTML (scaled, themed) |\n| 23 | About & Credits | paged: Source Information · Testing Feedback · Engine Feedback · Prompt Feedback · Credits & engine changes · Controls |\n| 24 | Dev panel | pixel overlay (§13) |\n| 25 | Hub card / hub splash | §12.2 |\n\nTransitions: `game.go(scene, data, { fade: 'wipe' })` between menu screens, `'iris'` into events, `'white'` into\nceremonies (reduce-motion turns them into plain fades by itself).\n\n### 3.3 Touch-only devices\n\n`touchOnly = matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches\n&& navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0` **and** no key press or gamepad has been seen this visit. While true: show screen 4,\nhide engine touch buttons (scenes declare `touch: []`; set the engine's `touch` setting to `'off'` at boot),\nlet the attract mode run after 10 s; a tap during attract returns to the notice. A `gamepadconnected` event or any key\npress flips `touchOnly` to false at once and shows the normal title.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Architecture\n\n### 4.1 Tech stack\n\n- **Engine:** our copy of the my-3D2dge v0.85.2 full engine (`engine/my-3d2dge.js`: the core plus every module; we use\n  the `road`, `rhythm`, `physics`, `arcade` and `songs` modules from it). Plain JavaScript, no libraries, no CDN, no\n  network at runtime.\n- **Online:** AI Hub `session.js` 1.4.0, unmodified, first script in `<head>`.\n- **Tooling:** Node (headless tests via `vm`, as `game/test.mjs` does), Playwright 1.63.0 + Chromium (browser checks,\n  hub harness, screenshots), the handoff's `tools/check.mjs` and `tools/filmstrip.mjs`.\n- **Deliverable:** one self-contained HTML (engine + session client + game inlined), ~1.8 MB, works from `file://`.\n\n### 4.2 Folder layout\n\n```\nOlympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174837/\n  index.html                 dev page: same <head> as the bundle; loads engine + src files in order (the list is the bundle's source of truth)\n  engine/my-3d2dge.js        the engine + our changes, each marked  // OLY: <why>\n  vendor/hub/                session.js (bundled, unmodified) + harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md (tests only)\n  vendor/my-3d2dge/          sparse clone of the handoff: docs, kits, tools (reference + Playwright); never bundled\n  src/                       the game: one IIFE per file, all attach to window.OLY (alias O)\n  tests/                     run.mjs (the one command) + suites\n  tools/bundle.mjs           builds the root HTML from index.html's script list\n  tools/shots.mjs            screenshot/filmstrip helper around the handoff tools\n```\n\n### 4.3 Source files (load order = bundle order)\n\n| File | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| `00-core.js` | creates `window.OLY`, defines `OLY_KEYS` and `GAME_SETTINGS`, then the `E.Game` (§4.4), constants (`M`, palettes), `O.def(kind, id, spec)` registries, `O.bus` (on/emit), `O.store` (E.store wrappers), `O.rng(label)` streams (§4.7), formatting helpers |\n| `01-input.js` | per-scene presets (archery/curling/hockey add mouse), **pilots** (§5.2), `Mash` meter, `O.AutoPlayer` (bot intents → real `input.tap/hold`), idle/any-input tracker, touch-only detection, hub Create+Options guard |\n| `02-prompts.js` | `O.Prompt` glyph renderer (§2.3) |\n| `03-ui.js` | 10-foot widgets: `Menu10` (vertical list), `Grid10` (tile grid), `Tabs`, `Panel`, `Banner` (big stamped words), `Ticker`, `LowerThird`, `Toast`, page dots, number/time formatters; mouse hover/click via `input.pointer()`/`clicked()` |\n| `04-audio.js` | SFX definitions (`audio.define`), songs (§9), `O.Voice` gibberish synth, `O.CrowdAudio`, `O.Anthem` generator, stings |\n| `05-art.js` | palettes per venue, flag painter, pictograms, emblem, equipment sprites, stands + crowd painter, jumbotron (dot matrix), podium, fireworks/confetti helpers, mascot (Blob) |\n| `06-pose.js` | `O.Pose` library and `O.Gear` (equipment drawn at joints), body rotation (§8.2) |\n| `07-athletes.js` | `NATIONS` data (§7.1), teammates, `O.makeRig(nation, sport, opts)` outfits per sport, voice params |\n| `08-commentary.js` | `O.Comms`: commentators, line banks (§7.6), cue rules, subtitles + voice + portraits |\n| `09-hype.js` | `O.Hype` (meter, turbo), `O.Yikes` (stamp, count, sympathy hype), `O.Crowd` (visual excitement), slow-mo/photo helpers |\n| `10-records.js` | world records seed table, games records, personal bests, top-5 tables via `E.Arcade`, points formulas, medal table, party points, laurels, cabinet |\n| `11-ghosts.js` | ghost recording/playback (local turns + hub), compact samples |\n| `12-eventkit.js` | the event contract, `O.defEvent`, the phase runner, formats (race / attempts / match / program), CPU simulation, results assembly, qualifying, timing widgets (`TimingBar`, `Ring`, `Balance`, `Sway`, `StrokeBeat`) |\n| `20-ev-sprint.js` … `35-ev-hockey.js` | one file per event (§6), ids: `sprint javelin swim diving archery weights skijump downhill bobsled figure curling hockey` |\n| `40-sc-title.js` | title, main menu, touch-only notice, attract director (§11) |\n| `41-sc-games.js` | games setup, nation select/join, opening ceremony, day card, story beats, event card, turn card, results, initials, medal ceremony, medal table, torch/continue, closing + ending + credits |\n| `42-sc-misc.js` | single event select, records, how to play, about & credits, pose lab |\n| `43-dev.js` | dev panel, tuning sliders, cheats, overlays (§13) |\n| `44-hub.js` | AI Hub session flow (§12.2) |\n| `99-main.js` | registers scenes, deep links (`#ev/<id>`, `#scene/<name>`, `#attract`, `#poselab`, `#games/day/<n>`), `game.debug`, `window.__oly` for tests, `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot })` |\n\nEach file: `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge, O = OLY; ... })();`. Tests load exactly the same files in the same\norder into a `vm` context (read the list from `index.html` so it can't drift).\n\n### 4.4 The game object\n\n```js\nconst game = O.game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'brawler', input: OLY_KEYS,\n  id: 'olympiyikes', bg: '#0b0f24',\n  menu: { items: inHub ? [] : [['Restart event', () => O.restartEvent()], ['How to play', () => O.showEventCard()], ['Dev mode', () => O.Dev.open()]],\n          settings: GAME_SETTINGS /* §2.5 */, quit: inHub ? false : 'title',\n          controls: { mashL: 'Left (mash, pass, tuck)', mashR: 'Right (mash, check, pike)', action: 'Action (jump, throw, release)',\n                      turbo: 'Yikes Turbo', alt: 'Hold (tuck, breathe, steady)' } } });\n```\n`inHub = !!(window.AIHub && AIHub.active)` (true from the client's first line). `OLY_KEYS` and `GAME_SETTINGS` are defined\nin `00-core.js` above this call (the later files only add to them); the menu callbacks are arrows because their targets\nare defined in later files. Menu items are static, so each item's\nfunction is context-aware: outside an event \"Restart event\" and \"How to play\" show `game.note('Not in an event')`.\n\n### 4.5 Scenes\n\n`O.scene(name, def)` registers a wrapped engine scene. The wrapper: calls `input.consumeAll()` on enter; applies the\n0.6 s lockout (`def.lock`); if the dev panel is open, runs `O.Dev.update()` instead of `def.update` and draws the panel\nafter `def.draw`; draws global overlays (toasts, commentary lower third, attract banner, \"YIKES\" stamps, laurel toasts)\nafter the scene; tracks idle time on menu scenes (`def.menu: true`) for the attract mode; routes `back()`.\n\nScenes: `title`, `menu`, `touchonly` (a state of `title`), `attract`, `gamesSetup`, `nations`, `opening`, `dayCard`,\n`beat`, `eventCard`, `turnCard` (overlay state inside events), `ev:<id>` ×12, `results`, `initials`, `podium`,\n`medals`, `torch`, `closing`, `credits`, `single`, `records`, `howto`, `about`, `poselab`, `hubCard`, `hubSplash`.\n\n`O.flow` is the state machine above them: it holds the current **meet** (§4.8) and decides the next scene after each\none ends (`O.flow.next(from, payload)`), for each mode (games, quick, single, hub, attract).\n\n### 4.6 Engine changes (all marked `// OLY:` in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`; list them on the About screen)\n\n1. **Export the hooks:** `E.HOOKS = HOOKS;` so the game can run code at the start of every step even while paused\n   (`HOOKS.input`): hub Create+Options guard, global any-input detection.\n2. **Cosmetic randomness off `Math.random`:** the Humanoid (`this.t` start phase, blink timer), `Particles` and\n   `hitFx`'s number jitter draw from a module-level `const cosmetic = rng(0xC05E7)` instead of `Math.random`. Then\n   cosmetics never shift gameplay randomness, the hub card draws no `Math.random` numbers, and tests stay\n   deterministic.\n3. **Font glyphs:** add ✕ ○ □ △ to the 5×7 (`G57`) and 3×5 (`GLYPHS`) tables.\n4. **PlayStation by default:** the `glyphs` setting's default becomes `'playstation'`.\n5. **Pose hook on the Humanoid:** at the end of `solvePose()`, if `this.post` is a function call `this.post(this, this.J)`;\n   then, if `this.bodyPitch || this.bodyRoll`, rotate every joint (and cape/hair points, as `_roll` does) about\n   `this.pivot` (default: hip centre) by pitch (forward–up plane) and roll (right–up plane), **without** `_roll`'s\n   floor lift. This is what flips, dives, swims, tumbles and V-style flight need (§8.2).\n6. **Voice bus:** `ChipAudio` gets a `_voiceBus` gain under the master, a `voiceVolume` field honored by\n   `applyVolume()`, and the settings get `voices` (Sound group, default .8). `O.Voice` connects to it.\n\n### 4.7 Randomness and determinism\n\n- **Gameplay randomness only through labeled streams:** `O.rng(label)` returns a `[0,1)` generator. In a hub session\n  it is `AIHub.rng(label)` (identical on every copy); otherwise `E.rng(hash(meet.seed + '|' + label))`. Labels are\n  namespaced: `'sprint:gun'`, `'javelin:wind:r2'`, `'cpu:FRM:skijump:j1'`, `'judges:diving:SUN:d3'`.\n- Cosmetics (particles, blinks, crowd bob) never touch `Math.random` (engine change 2) or the gameplay streams.\n- `meet.seed` comes from the clock when a meet starts; tests and the dev panel can set it.\n- Every event sim is a function of (state, intents, streams), independent of drawing, so a sim can run headless,\n  twice, and hash the same (the kits' \"proof\" idea).\n\n### 4.8 Data model\n\n```\nNation   { id: 'SUN', name: 'SUNVALE', colors: { a, b, c }, flag: { kind, ... }, athlete: { name, rig, bio, voice },\n           team: [3 names], skills: { sprint: .92, ... }, anthem: { seed, key, bpm } }\nAthlete  { id, nation, name, rig, gear, pilot, lane, human, player (0-3 | null), hype, turboT, state: {}, attempts: [],\n           best, notes: [], ghost }\nPilot    { kind: 'human' | 'bot' | 'ghost' | 'net', input?, skill, cheat?: 'perfect' | null }\nIntent   { L, R, A, T (pressed this step), Lh, Rh, Ad, Au, X (held / released), mv: [x, y], aim: [x, y] | null }","numLines":400,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1723}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0177jky3TvTUDzRoBkSwqMw8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
